        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is a clean, additive slice that mirrors the patterns already established in this codebase:\n\n- **`ClinicServiceImpl.exceedsBulkSignupThreshold`** is a near-exact structural twin of the existing `rejectWhenDailyLimitReached` \u2014 same `day` resolution (registration date, defaulting to `LocalDate.now()`) and it reuses the shared `countOwnersRegisteredOn` helper rather than duplicating the counting logic. The `BULK_SIGNUP_WARNING_THRESHOLD` constant is declared inline next to its consumer, matching the existing convention for `DAILY_OWNER_LIMIT` and `CITY_CAPACITY`. The computation is correctly placed in the service layer alongside the other pre-persist accumulation rules, and wired into `createOwner` next to the analogous `namesakeCount` assignment.\n- **`Owner`** gets a plain field + getter/setter identical in shape to `namesakeCount`.\n- **`OwnerMapper.bulkSignupWarning`** follows the same default-method-per-derived-field idiom as `displayName`, `membershipTier`, `locality`, etc., including the null-owner guard.\n- Schema changes are applied consistently across all four DB dialects, and the OpenAPI field is `readOnly` like its siblings.\n\nLogic sits in the right units (persistence-time decision in the service, presentation mapping in the mapper), nothing is duplicated, and no class is turning into a catch-all. A maintainer would find this natural."
